---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { EDITOR_AUTHOR } from '../../lib/editorial';
import { ENGINE_VERSION } from '../../lib/engine/types';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'How zodiacs.org computes your chart',
    url: 'https://zodiacs.org/methodology/',
    datePublished: '2026-07-05T10:36:57Z',
    dateModified: '2026-07-31T19:00:00Z',
    author: EDITOR_AUTHOR,
    publisher: { '@id': 'https://zodiacs.org/#org' },
    mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': 'https://zodiacs.org/methodology/' },
    inLanguage: 'en',
    about: 'Astronomical methodology, timezone handling, and privacy model of the zodiacs.org chart engine.',
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="How We Compute Your Chart — Methodology & Privacy | Zodiacs.org"
  description="How zodiacs.org computes a chart: ephemeris and house methods, JPL Horizons comparison tests, host-provided historical time zones, honest limits, and local-first privacy."
  path="/methodology/"
  type="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="section container container--narrow">
    <em class="kicker">Methodology</em>
    <h1 class="method-title display">How we calculate your chart.</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Here is what a chart on zodiacs.org claims, how each part is
        calculated, and where its precision has honest limits.
      </p>

      <h2>The ephemeris</h2>
      <p>
        Planetary positions come from <a href="https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astronomy Engine</a>,
        an open-source astronomical library accurate to about one arcminute
        (a sixtieth of a degree). We compute <strong>apparent geocentric
        tropical longitudes of date</strong> — the standard frame of Western
        astrology — including light-time, aberration, and nutation. The Moon
        uses a dedicated high-accuracy lunar model; the lunar node is the{' '}
        <em>true node</em>, derived from the Moon's instantaneous orbit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every change to the engine must pass an automated accuracy gate
        before it ships. Committed vectors from{' '}
        <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/" rel="noopener noreferrer">JPL Horizons</a>{' '}
        cover modern and historical epochs, including 1907. In those vectors,
        the tolerance is 0.05° for most planets and up to 0.2° for the Moon.
        This is a reproducible comparison test, not certification or endorsement
        by NASA or JPL. The suite also checks the sky against itself:
        at sunrise the computed ascendant must match the Sun's position; at
        solar noon the midheaven must.
      </p>

      <h2>Angles and houses</h2>
      <p>
        The ascendant and midheaven are computed from Greenwich apparent
        sidereal time, your birthplace coordinates, and the mean obliquity of the
        ecliptic used by engine version {ENGINE_VERSION} (IAU 2006 polynomial). We offer <strong>whole sign</strong> houses (the
        oldest system — each house is one complete sign) and{' '}
        <strong>Placidus</strong> (the most common modern system, computed by
        iterative semi-arc division). Placidus is mathematically undefined
        near the poles; above 66° latitude we fall back to whole sign and say
        so on the chart.
      </p>

      <h2>Progressions, returns, and composites</h2>
      <p>
        Secondary progressions use the day-for-a-year method: one civil day after
        birth stands for one tropical year (365.2422 days) of life. We progress the
        planets only — progressed house angles require a convention we haven't
        adopted, so we don't show them. A solar-return chart is a full chart cast
        for the instant the Sun returns to its natal longitude, accurate to the
        same standard as every other chart here. A composite chart takes the
        shorter-arc midpoint of each pair of matching planets across two charts;
        composite houses require a location convention we won't fake, so composite
        charts are shown without houses.
      </p>

      <h2>Time zones</h2>
      <p>
        A birth chart is only as good as its clock conversion. We resolve your
        birth time with the <strong>IANA/ICU time-zone history supplied by your
        browser or host runtime</strong>: daylight saving, regional and wartime
        clock changes, and—where the runtime supplies it—pre-standardization{' '}
        <em>local mean time</em>. One committed test covers Mexico City's
        −6:36:36 offset in 1907.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Skipped times</strong> (spring-forward gaps) shift forward across the gap — the standard convention — and the chart says so.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeated times</strong> (fall-back folds) use the earlier pass, flagged on the chart.</li>
        <li><strong>Very old dates</strong> use the local mean time recorded for your birthplace's reference zone when the host runtime provides it. Before civil time standardization, records are less uniform and a chart can differ by a few clock minutes from tools that use the exact town meridian—we flag these charts too.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every result lists its computed UTC instant, so the conversion is
        yours to check.
      </p>

      <h2>What “accurate” means here</h2>
      <p>
        Astronomical accuracy means the positions, angles, and clock conversion
        are reproducible within the stated inputs and test tolerances. It does
        not establish that astrological interpretation or prediction is
        scientifically validated. Interpretive text is a clearly labeled
        astrological convention; it is not an astronomical measurement.
      </p>

      <h2>How to verify a result</h2>
      <p>Open “How this chart was calculated” beneath a result and compare:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the birth date, local time, place, and coordinates used;</li>
        <li>the computed UTC instant and any daylight-saving or local-mean-time flag;</li>
        <li>the selected house system and any polar fallback; and</li>
        <li>the displayed engine version before comparing another tool.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Birthplace search</h2>
      <p>
        Place lookup runs against a bundled index of ~34,000 cities from{' '}
        <a href="https://www.geonames.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GeoNames</a>{' '}
        (CC BY 4.0), each carrying its IANA time zone. Coordinates are stored
        to ~1 km precision — far finer than a birth chart needs.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        The entire calculation happens in your browser. Your birth date, time,
        and place are not sent to a chart API, and there is no account
        requirement or analytics attached to birth data. Charts you save live
        in your browser's local storage first, deletable by you at any time. If
        you choose account sync, saved charts upload to your Supabase account
        and are protected by row-level security.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Chiron and the asteroids aren't included yet (the engine's remit is major bodies); they're planned via public-domain JPL data.</li>
        <li>Unknown birth times get a noon reference chart. Rising sign and houses are omitted, and dates when the Moon may have changed signs are flagged.</li>
        <li>Historical time-zone coverage comes from the visitor's IANA/ICU runtime. Pre-1970 civil-time records can be incomplete, disputed, or differ between runtime versions.</li>
        <li>Supported birth years: 1800–2199.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="daily-publication">Daily publication system</h2>
      <p>
        Zodiacs.org is maintained through automated editorial systems rather
        than a traditional human editorial staff. A model is not allowed to
        invent a sky fact or approve its own output. The daily system starts with a
        deterministic noon-UTC snapshot: body positions, lunar phase, and any
        exact events covered by that month’s committed transit file. Missing
        event coverage is labeled as unavailable rather than presented as a
        quiet day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today’s short readings are rendered from versioned local sentence
        templates. Every published line carries stable evidence IDs for its
        source position or event and, where relevant, the derived solar house.
        A separately implemented astronomy audit recomputes positions, lunar
        state, event geometry, and the monthly day slice. A deterministic copy
        check then rebuilds all twelve readings, checks their IDs, rejects unsafe
        certainty and restricted advice, tests sign-to-sign distinctness, and
        compares hashes. That copy check intentionally reuses the versioned
        renderer and is not described as an independent second implementation.
        The same process is replayed across the previous 30 days on every run.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those evidence IDs establish the astronomical input and the derived
        whole-sign solar house. Phrases about a house’s life themes are
        versioned editorial conventions, not astronomical measurements; the
        template ID identifies that interpretive layer instead of disguising it
        as an observed fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI agents may propose new templates or a constrained selection system in
        a future edition, but free-form model text cannot pass the current release
        gate. The <a href="/data/editorial-constitution.json">editorial constitution</a>
        is public. The exact policy version, generation mode, source hashes, and
        verification result for the live edition are available in the{' '}
        <a href="/data/daily-publication.json">daily publication record</a>.
        Material fixes appear in the <a href="/corrections/">correction log</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Found a discrepancy? We treat accuracy reports as bugs. The sky is
        checkable, and so is our arithmetic.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .method-title {
    margin: 14px 0 24px;
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    line-height: 1.04;
  }
</style>
